Unlike kubectl apply, which creates or updates resources declaratively, kubectl create is strictly imperative and will fail with an AlreadyExists error if the resource is already running in your cluster.


Common CLI Examples

• Create a Namespace: kubectl create namespace dev
• Create a Deployment: kubectl create deployment nginx --image=nginx --replicas=3
• Create a Service: kubectl create service clusterip my-svc --tcp=80:8080
• Create a ConfigMap: kubectl create configmap app-config --from-literal=ENV=prod
• Create a Secret: kubectl create secret generic db-pass --from-

One of the most practical uses of kubectl create is generating clean YAML boilerplates without actually creating anything in your cluster. Combine --dry-run=client and -o yaml to save the output to a file:

kubectl create deployment nginx --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml > nginx-deploy.yaml
