kubectl scale deployment nginx replicas=5
#for scaling replicas in deployment

The standard format is kubectl scale --replicas=<count> <resource-type>/<resource-name>.

• Scale up a Deployment: kubectl scale --replicas=5 deployment/nginx (Creates 5 running pods of nginx).

• Scale down to zero: kubectl scale --replicas=0 deployment/nginx (Stops all pods while keeping the deployment configuration intact).

• Scale multiple deployments at once: kubectl scale --replicas=3 deployment/nginx deployment/redis


Useful Flags

• --current-replicas=<count>: Acts as a safety check. Kubernetes will only perform the scaling operation if the current number of replicas matches the number you specify (e.g., kubectl scale --current-replicas=2 --replicas=4 deployment/nginx).
• --all: Scales all resources of the specified type within a namespace (e.g., kubectl scale --replicas=0 deployment --all

When you run kubectl scale on a ReplicaSet or deployment created from a configuration file, it immediately updates the live number of running pods in the cluster, while leaving your local YAML file completely unchanged.


What Happens When You Re-apply the File Later

Because your local file and the live cluster are now out of sync, your next action depends on which command you use to update the file later:

• kubectl apply -f <file>.yaml: Kubernetes overwrites your manual scaling(using kubectl scale) and resets the live ReplicaSet back to the original replica count written in your YAML file.

• kubectl replace -f <file>.yaml: Kubernetes also resets the live ReplicaSet back to the count in your YAML file, and may throw an error or warning if the resource metadata conflicts.

• Deleting and Recreating: If you delete the ReplicaSet and run kubectl create -f <file>.yaml, it will launch with the original replica count from the file.

One Crucial Caveat (Deployments vs. ReplicaSets)

If your ReplicaSet is managed by a Deployment rather than created as a standalone ReplicaSet, running kubectl scale directly on the ReplicaSet will not stick. The Deployment controller continuously monitors its ReplicaSets and will immediately revert your manual scaling back to whatever replica count is set on the Deployment. In that case, you must scale the Deployment instead (kubectl scale deployment/<name>).

Important Note on Autoscaling

If you have a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) attached to your deployment, any manual changes made with kubectl scale will be temporary. The HPA will automatically override your manual replica count within a few minutes based on current CPU or memory usage.

