The command kubectl set directly updates specific, targeted fields of existing Kubernetes resources from the command line without opening a text editor or modifying YAML files.

It works as an imperative shortcut for common updates like changing a container image, adding environment variables, or adjusting CPU and memory limits.

Most Common Subcommands

• kubectl set image: Updates the container image for a workload, triggering a rolling update on Deployments.
	• Example: kubectl set image deployment/nginx nginx=nginx:1.25

• kubectl set env: Adds, updates, or removes environment variables on containers.
	• Example: kubectl set env deployment/nginx ENV=production
	• Remove a variable: kubectl set env deployment/nginx ENV- (using a trailing minus sign)

• kubectl set resources: Configures CPU and memory requests or limits for containers.
	• Example: kubectl set resources deployment/nginx --limits=cpu=200m,memory=512Mi

• kubectl set serviceaccount: Changes the ServiceAccount assigned to a workload's pods.
	• Example: kubectl set serviceaccount deployment/nginx my-service-account

• kubectl set selector: Updates the label selector on a Service.
	• Example: kubectl set selector service/nginx app=nginx-v2

Why Use It Over kubectl edit

• Automation Friendly: Because it is a single, non-interactive command, you can easily embed kubectl set inside CI/CD pipelines and shell scripts.

• Safer for Targeted Changes: It modifies only the exact field you specify, avoiding accidental formatting errors or unintended edits inside a live YAML file.

• Configuration Drift Warning: Just like kubectl edit and kubectl scale, any changes made with kubectl set only affect the live cluster and will not update your local YAML files.

