The command kubectl replace updates an existing Kubernetes resource by completely overwriting its live configuration with a new specification from a file or standard input.

Unlike kubectl apply, which merges changes cleanly, kubectl replace is strictly imperative and requires the target resource to already exist in the cluster.

--
Basic Syntax & Usage

• Standard Replace: kubectl replace -f deployment.yaml (Overwrites the live deployment with the exact contents of deployment.yaml).

• Force Replace (Delete and Recreate): kubectl replace --force -f pod.yaml 

(Deletes the existing resource created using that file completely and creates a brand-new one as per the file).
--
• Fails if Missing: If the resource does not already exist in the cluster, the command fails with a NotFound error.
